<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The rational cohomology of the mapping class group
<br class="ltx_break">vanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text">The authors gratefully acknowledge support from the National Science Foundation.</span></span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Thomas Church, Benson Farb and Andrew Putman
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the mapping class group of a genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> surface. The
group <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> has virtual cohomological dimension <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math>. In this note we use a theorem of
Broaddus and the combinatorics of chord diagrams to prove that <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the mapping class group of a closed, oriented, genus <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> surface, and let <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the moduli space of genus <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> Riemann surfaces. It is well-known that for each <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong H^{i}({\mathcal{M}}_{g};\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong H^{i}({\mathcal{M}}_{g};\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is a fundamental open problem to determine the maximal <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for which these vector
spaces are nonzero. Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha</a>]</cite> proved that the <em id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">virtual cohomological dimension</em>
<math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, he proved that <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.372em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
for a certain <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (see below for details) and
that <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\Mod_{g};V\otimes\mathbb{Q})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\Mod_{g};V\otimes\mathbb{Q})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;4g-5</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the first step of the problem above is to determine whether <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The purpose of this note is to answer this question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>) denote the mapping class group of the genus <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface with one marked point (resp. one boundary component).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})=H^{4g-5}({\mathcal{M}}_{g};\mathbb{Q})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})=H^{4g-5}({\mathcal{M}}_{g};\mathbb{Q})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Further, the rational cohomology of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. the integral cohomology of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>) vanishes in its virtual cohomological dimension.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem was announced some years ago by Harer, but he has informed us that his proof will not appear. We recently learned that Morita–Sakasai–Suzuki <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS</a>]</cite> have independently found a proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> using a completely different method. They apply a theorem of Kontsevich on graph homology to their computation of a generating set for a certain symplectic Lie algebra. Our proof combines some results about
the combinatorics of chord diagrams with the work of Broaddus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>]</cite>
on the Steinberg module of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We thank Allen Hatcher and Takuya Sakasai for their comments on an earlier version of this paper, and John Harer for informing us about the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS</a>]</cite> and his own work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is consistent with the well-studied analogy between mapping class groups and arithmetic groups. For example, Theorem 1.3 of Lee–Szczarba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">LS</a>]</cite> states that the rational cohomology of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(n,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(n,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes in its cohomological dimension.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by briefly summarizing previous results that make our computation possible; for details see Broaddus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teichmüller space and its boundary.</span> Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected, closed orientable surface of genus <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">curve complex</em> of <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by Harvey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harv</a>]</cite>, i.e. the flag complex whose <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices are the <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of distinct free homotopy classes of simple closed curves in <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that can be realized disjointly. Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to a wedge of spheres <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigvee_{i=1}^{\infty}S^{2g-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigvee_{i=1}^{\infty}S^{2g-2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a constant <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that any two closed geodesics on a hyperbolic surface of length <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint (the <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Margulis constant</em> for hyperbolic surfaces). Let <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Teichmüller space of marked hyperbolic surfaces diffeomorphic to <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> having no closed geodesic of length <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. It is known that <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(6g-6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(6g-6)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional manifold with corners. Ivanov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iv</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible and that its boundary <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Briefly, for each simplex <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of
<math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of surfaces where each curve in <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each <math id="S2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible, and <math id="S2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\sigma}\cap\mathcal{T}_{\sigma^{\prime}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\sigma}\cap\mathcal{T}_{\sigma^{\prime}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\cup\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\cup\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a simplex of <math id="S2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case <math id="S2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\sigma}\cap\mathcal{T}_{\sigma^{\prime}}=\mathcal{T}_{\sigma\cup\sigma^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\sigma}\cap\mathcal{T}_{\sigma^{\prime}}=\mathcal{T}_{\sigma\cup\sigma^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Duality in the mapping class group.</span> The mapping class group <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> acts properly discontinuously on <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite stabilizers. Defining
<math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\thick}=\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}/\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\thick}=\mathcal{T}_{g}^{\thick}/\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that
<math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\thick};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\thick};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Mumford’s compactness criterion states that <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\thick}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathsize="1.280em">thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\thick}</annotation></semantics></math> is compact.
Combining this with the previous two paragraphs, the work of Bieri–Eckmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">BE</a>, Theorem 6.2]</cite> shows that
<math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{g})=4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{g})=4g-5</annotation></semantics></math> and that</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{0}(\Mod_{g};H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}_{g};\mathbb{Q}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\Mod_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{0}(\Mod_{g};H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}_{g};\mathbb{Q})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, we can say more. Let <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Steinberg module</em>, i.e. the <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}_{g};\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}_{g};\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.372em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the rational <em id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dualizing module</em> for <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5-k}(\Mod_{g};M\otimes\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{k}(\Mod_{g};M\otimes\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5-k}(\Mod_{g};M\otimes\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{k}(\Mod_{g};M\otimes\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is also the dualizing module for <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which act on <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> via the natural surjections <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,*}\to\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,*}\to\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}\to\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}\to\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha</a>]</cite>. This implies that for <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\vcd(\Mod_{g,*})=4g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\vcd(\Mod_{g,*})=4g-3</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\nu-k}(\Mod_{g,*};M\otimes\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{k}(\Mod_{g,*};M\otimes\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\nu-k}(\Mod_{g,*};M\otimes\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{k}(\Mod_{g,*};M\otimes\St_{g}\otimes\,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a similar result with <math id="S2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\cd(\Mod_{g,1})=4g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">cd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\cd(\Mod_{g,1})=4g-2</annotation></semantics></math>, except that since <math id="S2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is torsion-free the result holds integrally: <math id="S2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\nu-k}(\Mod_{g,1};M)\cong H_{k}(\Mod_{g,1};M\otimes\St_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\nu-k}(\Mod_{g,1};M)\cong H_{k}(\Mod_{g,1};M\otimes\St_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An alternate model for <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Fix a finite-volume hyperbolic metric on <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}-\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}-\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Another model for <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the <em id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arc complex</em> <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the flag complex whose <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices are the disjoint <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of simple geodesics on <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}-\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}-\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math> beginning and ending at the cusp <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subcomplex consisting of collections of geodesics <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-\bigcup\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>⋃</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-\bigcup\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has some non-contractible component. Harer proved that <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha</a>]</cite>, and that <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha2</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hat</a>]</cite>). Thus</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}=H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}_{g})\simeq H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty})\simeq H_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}_{g}/\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}=H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{C}_{g})\simeq H_{2g-2}(\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty})\simeq H_{2g-1}(\mathcal{A}_{g}/\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chord diagrams.</span> By examining how the geodesics are arranged in a neighborhood of <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex of <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> can be encoded by a <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagram; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>, §4.1]</cite>. An <em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ordered <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagram</em> is an ordered sequence <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an unordered pair of
distinct points on <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (a <em id="S2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chord</em>) and
<math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}\cap u_{j}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}\cap u_{j}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will visually depict <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> by drawing arcs connecting
the points in each <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for examples).
Two ordered chord diagrams are identified if they differ by an orientation-preserving
homeomorphism of the circle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filling systems.</span> An unlabeled <em id="S2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system</em> of genus <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+k)</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagram satisfying the conditions described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>, §4.1]</cite>: no chord should be parallel to another chord or to the boundary circle, and the chords should determine exactly <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> boundary cycles. These conditions, which guarantee that these chords define a simplex of <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{g}-\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{g}-\mathcal{A}_{g}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, have the following simple combinatorial formulation. Given <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, consider two permutations of the <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}\cup\cdots\cup u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}\cup\cdots\cup u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>: let <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle which takes each point to the point immediately adjacent in the clockwise direction, while <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> exchanges the two points of each chord <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus is a product of <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> transpositions. Then a <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+k)</annotation></semantics></math>-chord diagram is a <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system of genus <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\circ\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\circ\omega</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> orbits, none of which have length 1 or 2. Finally, let <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the straight line in <math id="S2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> connecting the two points of <math id="S2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we say that <math id="S2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p8.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">disconnected</em> if the set <math id="S2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}\cup\cdots\cup t_{n}\subset D^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cup\cdots\cup t_{n}\subset D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The chord diagram chain complex.</span>
Fix a genus <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2g+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2g+k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the free abelian group spanned by ordered <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling systems of genus
<math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> modulo the following relation. For <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\cdot U=(u_{\sigma(1)},\ldots,u_{\sigma(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\cdot U=(u_{\sigma(1)},\ldots,u_{\sigma(n)})</annotation></semantics></math>. We impose the relation <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\cdot U=(-1)^{\sigma}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\cdot U=(-1)^{\sigma}U</annotation></semantics></math>.
The differential <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial:\mathcal{U}_{k}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial:\mathcal{U}_{k}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows. Consider
an ordered <math id="S2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{i}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}U</annotation></semantics></math> equal <math id="S2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{1},\ldots,\widehat{u_{i}},\ldots,u_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{1},\ldots,\widehat{u_{i}},\ldots,u_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> if this is an ordered
<math id="S2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system of genus <math id="S2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, let <math id="S2.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{i}U=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}U=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial(U)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i-1}\partial_{i}U." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial(U)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i-1}\partial_{i}U.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broaddus’s results.</span>
We will need the following theorem of Broaddus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>]</cite>. Recall that if <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a group
and <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module, then the <em id="S2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">module of coinvariants</em>, denoted <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, is
the quotient <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M/\langle g\cdot m-m\,|\,g\in\Gamma,m\in M\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.150em" rspace="0.317em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/\langle g\cdot m-m\,|\,g\in\Gamma,m\in M\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system of genus <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Broaddus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\St_{g})_{\Mod_{g}}\cong\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.280em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\St_{g})_{\Mod_{g}}\cong\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The abelian group </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is spanned by the image </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X]\in\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X]\in\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a disconnected </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-filling system of genus </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then the image
of </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">For part (i) of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Broaddus []). ‣ 2. Background ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>, Proposition 3.3]</cite> together with the
remark preceding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>, Example 4.1]</cite>; for part (ii), see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>, Theorem 4.2]</cite>; and for part (iii), see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Br</a>, Proposition 4.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any group <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, recall that <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}(\Gamma;M)=M_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}(\Gamma;M)=M_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the actions of <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\St_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\St_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> factor through <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, to
prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> it suffices by (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Background ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to show that <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\St_{g})_{\Mod_{g}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">St</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.280em">Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\St_{g})_{\Mod_{g}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Broaddus []). ‣ 2. Background ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(i), this is equivalent to showing that
<math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[v]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the associated element of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}/\partial(\mathcal{U}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a).
By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Broaddus []). ‣ 2. Background ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(ii), it is enough to show that <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g},y)</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b).
Observe that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}Y=(x_{2},\ldots,x_{2g},y)=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})=X," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}Y=(x_{2},\ldots,x_{2g},y)=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})=X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the second equality holds since the indicated chord diagrams differ by
an orientation preserving homeomorphism of <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2g+1}Y=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2g+1}Y=X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}Y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}Y=0</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2g}Y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2g}Y=0</annotation></semantics></math>) by definition, since the chord <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2g+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2g+1}</annotation></semantics></math>) becomes parallel to the boundary. We thus have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial(Y)=2X+\sum_{i=3}^{2g-1}(-1)^{i-1}\partial_{i}Y." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial(Y)=2X+\sum_{i=3}^{2g-1}(-1)^{i-1}\partial_{i}Y.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq i\leq 2g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq i\leq 2g-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{i}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}Y</annotation></semantics></math> is disconnected, so Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Broaddus []). ‣ 2. Background ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(iii) implies that <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\partial_{i}Y]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\partial_{i}Y]=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that
<math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2[X]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2[X]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S3.F1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="/html/1108.0622/assets/ChordDiagrams.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="27180" height="8100"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>(a) The oriented <math id="S3.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S3.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})</annotation></semantics></math>. For concreteness, we depict
it for <math id="S3.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, <math id="S3.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> chords arranged in the same pattern as the chords shown.
<br class="ltx_break">(b) The <math id="S3.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S3.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g},y)</annotation></semantics></math>. The chord <math id="S3.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the chord <math id="S3.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">(c) The <math id="S3.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S3.F1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=(z,x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=(z,x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})</annotation></semantics></math>. The chord <math id="S3.F1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> intersects both <math id="S3.F1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider the <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-filling system <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=(z,x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=(z,x_{1},\ldots,x_{2g})</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c).
Removing any chord from Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c) yields Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem ‣ The rational cohomology of the mapping class groupvanishes
in its virtual cohomological dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a) up to rotation,
so <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{i}Z=\pm X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}Z=\pm X</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, it is clear that <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{1}Z=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1}Z=X</annotation></semantics></math>, that <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{2}Z=-X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{2}Z=-X</annotation></semantics></math>, that
<math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{3}Z=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{3}Z=X</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on, with <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{i}Z=(-1)^{i-1}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}Z=(-1)^{i-1}X</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial(Z)=X+X+\cdots X=(2g+1)X," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial(Z)=X+X+\cdots X=(2g+1)X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">so <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g+1)[X]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g+1)[X]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Summing up, we have shown that <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2[X]=(2g+1)[X]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2[X]=(2g+1)[X]=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.</p>
</div>
</section>
